Dear Yves,

This message is just for your reference in case you investigate the issue at some point. I have faced a similar problem when testing for measurement invariance for all loadings and thresholds in a two-group analysis of real (not simulated) data. The error message  I got for the PML estimator is

Error in solve.default(MinvHtM) :
  Lapack routine dgesv: system is exactly singular: U[9,9] = 0
In addition: Warning message:
In lavaan(model = PT.M1.extended, start = PE.M0.extended, control = list(optim.method = "none",  :
  lavaan WARNING: some estimated variances are negative

and for ULS and DWLS estimators is the same as that mentioned above:

Error in solve.default(A %*% P.inv %*% t(A)) :

  Lapack routine dgesv: system is exactly singular: U[9,9] = 0
I don't know if the following is related to the error messages but I'm reporting it in the case it helps. Using the command lavInspect(fit, "cov.all") I didn't see any negative variances in any of the methods but using the command cov2cor( lavInspect(fit, "cov.all") ) I noticed that in one of the groups the model-implied correlation matrix of observed and latent variables has a couple of correlations between some observed and latent variables larger than 1  (5 correlations, 2, and 4 for PML, ULS, DWLS, respectively).  However, for the same data when testing for measurement invariance for all loadings and the thresholds  of only one indicator, the lavTestLRT() command gives results for all three estimation methods despite the fact that in DWLS just one model-implied correlation between one observed and one latent variable is larger than 1.

Best regards,

Myrsini Katsikatsou

On Tuesday, 5 May 2015 07:51:10 UTC+1, yrosseel wrote:
> On 04/28/2015 01:31 AM, Galaxy Brown wrote:
> >>lavTestLRT(fit.model.teach.att, fit.model.teach.att1)
> > Error in solve.default(A %*% P.inv %*% t(A)) :
>
> This may be a bug in 0.5-18. I have seen it before, only in the
> categorical case. A workaround is to use:
>
> lavTestLRT(fit.model.teach.att, fit.model.teach.att1,
> method="Satorra.Bentler.2010")
>
> which gives you a mean-adjusted only difference test, but it will be in
> the ballpark.
>
> Yves.

