MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you're using std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE then the latent variables have means of 0 and variances (or residual variances) of 1.0. This is the key to all of your questions. It means that both the IV and the DV have means of 0 (the IV has a variance of 1.0, the DV has a residual variance of 1.0). So, having negative values of the DV makes perfect sense (it just means that the scores are below the mean). If you want to keep your variables in their original metric, I would recommend using effects coding as your method of scale setting.

In terms of what is "reasonable" in this situation, that's up to you. I would add that plotting from 0-4 on the X axis doesn't make a lot of sense (you're only plotting values of X that are above the mean of X, which strikes me as odd).

Alex

